% ============================================================================
% hurricane-category-home-damage — each Saffir-Simpson hurricane category
% (1..5) → the SPECIFIC well-built-home damage effect the source describes
% for it (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `hurricane-categories.adj`
% (`damage_level(category, descriptor)`, ONE generic damage word per
% category: some_damage, extensive_damage, devastating_damage,
% catastrophic_damage): a new `hurricane_home_damage(category,
% home_damage_effect)` table names the SPECIFIC structural effect the SAME
% NHC page describes a well-built framed home suffering at each category,
% decoded from spans already sitting unused inside `hurricane-categories.adj`'s
% own provenance block — no new WebFetch. That table's `descriptor` column
% has room for only ONE generic damage word per category; the SAME already-
% quoted NHC sentences also carry a distinct, more concrete structural detail
% for every category that never made it into a row:
%
%   category_1 → "damage to roof, shingles, vinyl siding and gutters"
%   category_2 → "major roof and siding damage"
%   category_3 → "major damage or removal of roof decking and gable ends"
%   category_4 → "severe damage with loss of most of the roof structure
%                  and/or some exterior walls"
%   category_5 → "total roof failure and wall collapse"
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? hurricane_home_damage(category_3, $HomeDamageEffect)
%       % major_damage_or_removal_of_roof_decking_and_gable_ends
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `hurricane-categories.adj`'s own `damage_level`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `hurricane_home_damage(category, home_damage_effect)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the structural effect WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of
% the five Saffir-Simpson categories (it never invents a damage effect).
%
% Unlike the parent table's descriptor column (which honestly DUPLICATES
% `catastrophic_damage` for both category_4 and category_5, since the source
% opens both paragraphs with the identical sentence), this table's five
% structural-effect values are all DISTINCT — the source's home-damage detail
% differs category by category even where the summary word repeats. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind an effect and recall which category it
% names:
%
%     ? hurricane_home_damage($Category, total_roof_failure_and_wall_collapse)
%       % category_5
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME five spans already quoted
% inside `hurricane-categories.adj`'s own provenance block, from the SAME
% NOAA / National Hurricane Center "Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale"
% page that table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema
% decoding a part of each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE
% provenance envelope (per `hurricane-categories.adj`'s own convention), so
% `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the category-1 span; every other
% row's own span is listed above for independent auditability. `trust
% authoritative` — the same tier `hurricane-categories.adj` already carries
% (NOAA / NHC, a primary U.S. government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table hurricane_home_damage {
    columns category, home_damage_effect

    row (category_1, damage_to_roof_shingles_vinyl_siding_and_gutters)
    row (category_2, major_roof_and_siding_damage)
    row (category_3, major_damage_or_removal_of_roof_decking_and_gable_ends)
    row (category_4, severe_damage_with_loss_of_most_of_the_roof_structure_and_or_some_exterior_walls)
    row (category_5, total_roof_failure_and_wall_collapse)

    source "Very dangerous winds will produce some damage: Well-constructed frame homes could have damage to roof, shingles, vinyl siding and gutters."
    locator "https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/aboutsshws.php"
    trust authoritative
}
